---
// "How to increase damage" + weapon selection + crit ratio — high-intent
// decision long-tail content.
---

<section id="increase-damage" class="page-shell section-pad scroll-mt-20 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
  <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)]">How to increase your damage</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    Players almost always farm artifacts first and level talents last — the exact reverse of the optimal order.
    Here is the priority path, ranked by damage gained per resin spent.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-forest-500/70">1</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Level your main talent to 9 or 10</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
            Each talent level is a guaranteed 6-7% multiplier on that skill — no RNG. Leveling a talent from 8 to 10 is
            often worth more than weeks of artifact farming. Do this before anything else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-forest-500/70">2</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Level to 90 for reaction &amp; DEF scaling</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
            For transformative and Quicken teams, going 80→90 raises reaction damage ~34%. For ATK/CRIT carries it is
            less critical (the DEF multiplier gain is small), but still helps against high-level enemies.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-forest-500/70">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Fix your Crit Ratio toward 1:2</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
            Rebalancing 50/200 into 70/160 is a ~6% gain from the same artifacts. Use the breakdown panel to see your
            current Crit multiplier and the advisor list to see what a Crit roll is worth on your build.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-forest-500/70">4</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Run an Anemo unit with Viridescent Venerer</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
            40% RES shred is a ~28% damage increase against most enemies — larger than almost any artifact upgrade.
            Kazuha, Sucrose or Venti holding VV belongs in every reaction team.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-forest-500/70">5</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Add Bennett or an equivalent buffer</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
            Bennett’s flat ATK buff is the single biggest common buff in the game. Noblesse 4pc (+20% ATK) and
            Thrilling Tales (+48% ATK on a catalyst support) are free additions on top.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-forest-500/70">6</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Farm artifacts last</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
            Artifact sub-stat farming has the worst return per resin because it is pure RNG. Once your talents, level,
            and team are set, farm for the correct main stats first, then refine sub-stats toward 1:2 Crit.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Weapon selection -->
  <h3 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)]">How to choose between two weapons</h3>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    The honest way to compare weapons is to change nothing but the weapon and watch the expected damage. Here is the
    framework the calculator follows:
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <h4 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">1. Secondary stat first</h4>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">A CRIT sub-stat weapon often beats a higher-base-ATK weapon, because CRIT is so scarce and hard to farm elsewhere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <h4 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">2. Passive must trigger</h4>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">A weapon whose passive you can’t reliably activate is just a stat stick. Read the trigger condition before assuming it works.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel p-5">
      <h4 class="font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">3. Scaling type matters</h4>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">HP- and DEF-scaling characters care far less about base ATK. Match the weapon to how the character actually scales.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    To compare directly: pick your character, set the first weapon, press <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">"Pin current
    as baseline"</strong>, then switch to the second weapon. The result panel shows the exact percentage gain or loss.
  </p>

  <!-- Crit ratio table -->
  <h3 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)]">The 1:2 Crit Ratio, in numbers</h3>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    Because a Crit Rate roll is worth half a Crit DMG roll, the optimal balance is always 1:2. Here is what that looks
    like in practice:
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)] text-[var(--muted)]">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Crit Rate</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Crit DMG</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">Crit multiplier (1 + CR×CD)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-[var(--text)]">
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">50%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">100%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 text-forest-600">1.50</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">60%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">120%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 text-forest-600">1.72</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">70%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">140%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 text-forest-600">1.98</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-[var(--line)]"><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">75%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">150%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 text-forest-600">2.13</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">80%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 pr-4">160%</td><td class="tnum py-2.5 text-forest-600">2.28</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    Notice how the multiplier climbs fastest when both stats rise together. A 60/120 build (1.72) beats a 50/140 build
    (1.70) despite the second looking flashier — this is why "more Crit DMG" is usually the wrong instinct.
  </p>
</section>
